@*
    A WARNED subsystem: the descriptor may declare it, the apply accepts it, and nothing runs
    (design §4.1; docs/architecture/admin-dashboard-review.md, F-6).

    Its counterpart is Refusal, and the two must never be drawn alike — somewhere the operator can stand
    against a control whose only possible output is a descriptor the apply rejects. That rule lives in
    these two components now rather than in whichever markup a screen copied: an alert in the information
    tone here (a state, never an error: the alert is role=status), an inert control with its reason there.

    The consequence is the framework's own sentence, served verbatim from capabilities and never
    reworded: a second wording is a third spelling of one truth.
*@
<div @attributes="Attributes" class="@CssClass.Of("a-notyet-frame", Attributes)">
    <AlvoAlert Tone="AlvoAlert.AlertTone.Info" Title="@Title" TestId="notyet-panel">
        @if (Subtitle is not null)
        {
            <div class="a-notyet-frame__sub">@Subtitle</div>
        }
        @if (Consequence is { Length: > 0 })
        {
            <NotYetConsequence Consequence="@Consequence" />
        }
    </AlvoAlert>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>What is not there yet, or what the operator needs to know about it.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>What declaring it does today.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Subtitle { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The framework's own sentence about what does not happen, shown verbatim.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Consequence { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Anything else, onto the panel's element.</summary>
    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? Attributes { get; set; }
}
